{"data":{"messageDelta":"lify","messageSoFar":"**Subject:** Weekly Digest – Vercel vs Netlify (Product & Pricing Updates June 11‑18 2026)\n\n---\n\n### Vercel\n\n#### Key Moves (last 7 days)\n- **June 17 2026 – Launch of **Eve**, an open‑source agent framework**  \n  - Provides durable execution, sandboxed compute, human‑in‑the‑loop approvals, sub‑agents, tracing, and evals.  \n  - Positioned as the “Next .js for AI agents”, enabling rapid build, test and deployment of production‑grade agents.  \n  - [vercel.com/blog/introducing-eve](https://vercel.com/blog/introducing-eve)  \n\n- **June 16 2026 – Announcement of **Vercel for Enterprise Apps & Agents**  \n  – Introduces **Vercel Passport** (default ID‑provider authentication), **Vercel Connect** (short‑lived scoped credentials to Slack, GitHub, Snowflake, etc.), and **Enterprise Managed Users**.  \n  – Adds optional “bring‑your‑own‑cloud” on AWS for full data‑plane control.  \n  - [vercel.com/blog/vercel-for-enterprise-apps-and-agents](https://vercel.com/blog/vercel-for-enterprise-apps-and-agents)  \n\n#### Pricing News (last 7 days)\n- No new Vercel pricing announcements were published in the June 11‑18 window. The most recent Pro‑plan overhaul (credit‑based model) dates to September 2025 and remains the current pricing structure.  \n\n#### Implications for Us\n- **Eve** signals a rapid expansion of Vercel’s AI‑agent ecosystem; we should evaluate whether their framework could accelerate our own internal agent development or present a competitive baseline for feature sets (durable sessions, sandboxing, built‑in approvals).  \n- **Enterprise Apps & Agents** tighten security and identity controls—if we aspire to sell enterprise‑grade AI tooling, we need comparable defaults (centralized SSO, scoped short‑lived tokens) to avoid a “security retrofit” after launch.  \n\n#### Sources\n1. Introducing Eve – Vercel Blog, June 17 2026.  \n2. Vercel for Enterprise Apps and Agents – Vercel Blog, June 16 2026.  \n\n---\n\n### Netlify\n\n#### Key Moves (last 7 days)\n- **June 9 2026 – Netlify added to the **Cursor marketplace** **  \n  - Developers can now connect their AI‑assisted coding environment (Cursor) directly to Netlify for one‑click deploys, reducing context switches.  \n  - [netlify.com/changelog/2026-06-09-netlify-cursor-marketplace/](https://www.netlify.com/changelog/2026-06-09-netlify-cursor-marketplace/)  \n\n- **June 9 2026 – Press release: **Netlify launches official MCP Server** **  \n  - The Model Context Protocol (MCP) server standardizes how AI agents interact with Netlify’s deployment, extensions, and site‑management APIs, enabling “agent‑native” development workflows.  \n  - [netlify.com/press/netlify-launches-official-mcp-server-setting-the-standard-for-agent-native-development/](https://www.netlify.com/press/netlify-launches-official-mcp-server-setting-the-standard-for-agent-native-development/)  \n\n#### Pricing News (last 7 days)\n- No Netlify pricing changes were announced in the June 11‑18 window. The most recent credit‑based pricing model was introduced in April 2026 and remains in effect.  \n\n#### Implications for Us\n- **Cursor marketplace integration** makes Netlify a more frictionless target for AI‑enhanced developer tools; we may need to ensure our tooling can either hook into Netlify’s new UI or offer comparable convenience.  \n- **MCP Server** formalizes AI‑agent access to the platform; if we plan to expose our own APIs to agents, adopting a similar protocol (or leveraging Netlify","sequence":0,"stepIndex":18,"turnId":"turn_0"},"type":"message.appended","meta":{"at":"2026-06-18T15:58:33.978Z"}}
{"data":{"messageDelta":"’s","messageSoFar":"**Subject:** Weekly Digest – Vercel vs Netlify (Product & Pricing Updates June 11‑18 2026)\n\n---\n\n### Vercel\n\n#### Key Moves (last 7 days)\n- **June 17 2026 – Launch of **Eve**, an open‑source agent framework**  \n  - Provides durable execution, sandboxed compute, human‑in‑the‑loop approvals, sub‑agents, tracing, and evals.  \n  - Positioned as the “Next .js for AI agents”, enabling rapid build, test and deployment of production‑grade agents.  \n  - [vercel.com/blog/introducing-eve](https://vercel.com/blog/introducing-eve)  \n\n- **June 16 2026 – Announcement of **Vercel for Enterprise Apps & Agents**  \n  – Introduces **Vercel Passport** (default ID‑provider authentication), **Vercel Connect** (short‑lived scoped credentials to Slack, GitHub, Snowflake, etc.), and **Enterprise Managed Users**.  \n  – Adds optional “bring‑your‑own‑cloud” on AWS for full data‑plane control.  \n  - [vercel.com/blog/vercel-for-enterprise-apps-and-agents](https://vercel.com/blog/vercel-for-enterprise-apps-and-agents)  \n\n#### Pricing News (last 7 days)\n- No new Vercel pricing announcements were published in the June 11‑18 window. The most recent Pro‑plan overhaul (credit‑based model) dates to September 2025 and remains the current pricing structure.  \n\n#### Implications for Us\n- **Eve** signals a rapid expansion of Vercel’s AI‑agent ecosystem; we should evaluate whether their framework could accelerate our own internal agent development or present a competitive baseline for feature sets (durable sessions, sandboxing, built‑in approvals).  \n- **Enterprise Apps & Agents** tighten security and identity controls—if we aspire to sell enterprise‑grade AI tooling, we need comparable defaults (centralized SSO, scoped short‑lived tokens) to avoid a “security retrofit” after launch.  \n\n#### Sources\n1. Introducing Eve – Vercel Blog, June 17 2026.  \n2. Vercel for Enterprise Apps and Agents – Vercel Blog, June 16 2026.  \n\n---\n\n### Netlify\n\n#### Key Moves (last 7 days)\n- **June 9 2026 – Netlify added to the **Cursor marketplace** **  \n  - Developers can now connect their AI‑assisted coding environment (Cursor) directly to Netlify for one‑click deploys, reducing context switches.  \n  - [netlify.com/changelog/2026-06-09-netlify-cursor-marketplace/](https://www.netlify.com/changelog/2026-06-09-netlify-cursor-marketplace/)  \n\n- **June 9 2026 – Press release: **Netlify launches official MCP Server** **  \n  - The Model Context Protocol (MCP) server standardizes how AI agents interact with Netlify’s deployment, extensions, and site‑management APIs, enabling “agent‑native” development workflows.  \n  - [netlify.com/press/netlify-launches-official-mcp-server-setting-the-standard-for-agent-native-development/](https://www.netlify.com/press/netlify-launches-official-mcp-server-setting-the-standard-for-agent-native-development/)  \n\n#### Pricing News (last 7 days)\n- No Netlify pricing changes were announced in the June 11‑18 window. The most recent credit‑based pricing model was introduced in April 2026 and remains in effect.  \n\n#### Implications for Us\n- **Cursor marketplace integration** makes Netlify a more frictionless target for AI‑enhanced developer tools; we may need to ensure our tooling can either hook into Netlify’s new UI or offer comparable convenience.  \n- **MCP Server** formalizes AI‑agent access to the platform; if we plan to expose our own APIs to agents, adopting a similar protocol (or leveraging Netlify’s","sequence":0,"stepIndex":18,"turnId":"turn_0"},"type":"message.appended","meta":{"at":"2026-06-18T15:58:33.994Z"}}
{"data":{"messageDelta":")","messageSoFar":"**Subject:** Weekly Digest – Vercel vs Netlify (Product & Pricing Updates June 11‑18 2026)\n\n---\n\n### Vercel\n\n#### Key Moves (last 7 days)\n- **June 17 2026 – Launch of **Eve**, an open‑source agent framework**  \n  - Provides durable execution, sandboxed compute, human‑in‑the‑loop approvals, sub‑agents, tracing, and evals.  \n  - Positioned as the “Next .js for AI agents”, enabling rapid build, test and deployment of production‑grade agents.  \n  - [vercel.com/blog/introducing-eve](https://vercel.com/blog/introducing-eve)  \n\n- **June 16 2026 – Announcement of **Vercel for Enterprise Apps & Agents**  \n  – Introduces **Vercel Passport** (default ID‑provider authentication), **Vercel Connect** (short‑lived scoped credentials to Slack, GitHub, Snowflake, etc.), and **Enterprise Managed Users**.  \n  – Adds optional “bring‑your‑own‑cloud” on AWS for full data‑plane control.  \n  - [vercel.com/blog/vercel-for-enterprise-apps-and-agents](https://vercel.com/blog/vercel-for-enterprise-apps-and-agents)  \n\n#### Pricing News (last 7 days)\n- No new Vercel pricing announcements were published in the June 11‑18 window. The most recent Pro‑plan overhaul (credit‑based model) dates to September 2025 and remains the current pricing structure.  \n\n#### Implications for Us\n- **Eve** signals a rapid expansion of Vercel’s AI‑agent ecosystem; we should evaluate whether their framework could accelerate our own internal agent development or present a competitive baseline for feature sets (durable sessions, sandboxing, built‑in approvals).  \n- **Enterprise Apps & Agents** tighten security and identity controls—if we aspire to sell enterprise‑grade AI tooling, we need comparable defaults (centralized SSO, scoped short‑lived tokens) to avoid a “security retrofit” after launch.  \n\n#### Sources\n1. Introducing Eve – Vercel Blog, June 17 2026.  \n2. Vercel for Enterprise Apps and Agents – Vercel Blog, June 16 2026.  \n\n---\n\n### Netlify\n\n#### Key Moves (last 7 days)\n- **June 9 2026 – Netlify added to the **Cursor marketplace** **  \n  - Developers can now connect their AI‑assisted coding environment (Cursor) directly to Netlify for one‑click deploys, reducing context switches.  \n  - [netlify.com/changelog/2026-06-09-netlify-cursor-marketplace/](https://www.netlify.com/changelog/2026-06-09-netlify-cursor-marketplace/)  \n\n- **June 9 2026 – Press release: **Netlify launches official MCP Server** **  \n  - The Model Context Protocol (MCP) server standardizes how AI agents interact with Netlify’s deployment, extensions, and site‑management APIs, enabling “agent‑native” development workflows.  \n  - [netlify.com/press/netlify-launches-official-mcp-server-setting-the-standard-for-agent-native-development/](https://www.netlify.com/press/netlify-launches-official-mcp-server-setting-the-standard-for-agent-native-development/)  \n\n#### Pricing News (last 7 days)\n- No Netlify pricing changes were announced in the June 11‑18 window. The most recent credit‑based pricing model was introduced in April 2026 and remains in effect.  \n\n#### Implications for Us\n- **Cursor marketplace integration** makes Netlify a more frictionless target for AI‑enhanced developer tools; we may need to ensure our tooling can either hook into Netlify’s new UI or offer comparable convenience.  \n- **MCP Server** formalizes AI‑agent access to the platform; if we plan to expose our own APIs to agents, adopting a similar protocol (or leveraging Netlify’s)","sequence":0,"stepIndex":18,"turnId":"turn_0"},"type":"message.appended","meta":{"at":"2026-06-18T15:58:34.011Z"}}
